<task id="132" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>C12 — access caches store fault-derived results and nothing invalidates them on BU/team/owner change: stop caching faults, add BFF write-path invalidation, shorten identity/membership TTLs, delete the dead roles/teams keys, and put the residual staleness in front of the owner</title>
    <phase>Register-driven (authored 2026-09-30, session 27; verified defect C12 from session 26)</phase>
    <gate>blocked until 131 and 109 are merged</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Authorization-path .cs changes across four caches (external grants, identity, membership, access snapshots) plus a shared-library type in Spaarke.Dataverse; tags bff-api/auth/security/testing.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Every fix here is a fail-direction decision: a "fault" and a "legitimate empty answer" look identical today, and each cache has a consumer for which the wrong classification is either a sticky outage or an over-grant. It also carries an ADR-009 compliance tension and an owner sign-off on residual staleness.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Edits ExternalParticipationService.cs (also 131, 109, 135 and 137 — C5 revocation/grant-cache invalidation), IdentityNormalizationService.cs (also 141 — C7 workforce identity binding — and 152), MembershipResolverService.cs and AccessibleRecordSetService.cs (also 036 — C9 owningbusinessunit removal + impersonated flag swap — and 152), ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs / UnsecureProjectEndpoint.cs (also 144, 148, 133, 139, 149, 150 — C10/C11 re-own and provisioning), and CachedAccessDataSource.cs / DataverseAccessDataSource.cs (read by 130 — C8 — whose new gate consumes the record-access snapshot; it must keep compiling and behaving the same under the additive fault indicator). Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/** is parallel-unsafe in this project.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>131, 109</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, security, caching, dataverse, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>2-3 days (five caches, a shared-library type, three Bicep stacks, per-entity eviction and a manual live gate)</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Session 26 verified defect C12 (notes/session26-uac-defects-and-synopsis.md § C12), CONFIRMED by 3 adversarial skeptics; tracked only informally (investigation 03 F5, task 070 W-7, investigation 02 §4.3 "must be an explicitly accepted risk" — never recorded). Owner round-2 decisions 2026-09-30 (notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md): no scope cuts, nothing deferred.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Four live caches cannot tell a failed read from a real answer, so a transient Dataverse fault (a 429, a timeout, a client abort) is stored as "this person has no grants / no teams / no rights" for 60 seconds to 15 minutes. Separately, no cache is invalidated when a user's business unit, teams or a record's owner change — those changes wait out the TTLs. Make every cache refuse to store a fault, make cancellation propagate, invalidate on the BFF's own ownership/team writes, shorten the identity and membership TTLs, delete the write-only roles/teams keys, and write down the staleness that remains so the owner can sign it off.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — the BFF's authorization data caches (external grants, membership identity/resolution, the Dataverse access-snapshot decorator). You are changing fail directions on the authorization boundary; for every change, name which way a wrong classification fails.</role>

  <goal>No access cache stores a result produced by a failed read; caller cancellation propagates and a timeout is treated as a fault; the identity, membership and impersonated-root-set caches (and the re-owned record's access snapshots) are invalidated by every BFF write that changes a user's teams/BU or a root record's owner — including writes made with no HttpContext — and the identity, membership and impersonated-root-set TTLs are shortened to the owner-approved bound; the dead roles/teams keys are gone; the access-snapshot cache complies with ADR-009 per the reviewed path; and the accepted residual staleness is documented and signed off by the owner.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>🔴 THE FOUR CACHES (verified against HEAD 2026-09-30 by this task's author; re-verify before editing — anchors drift).

(a) EXTERNAL GRANT SET — `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalParticipationService.cs`, 60 s TTL (`:18`). `QueryGrantSetAsync` returns `ExternalGrantSet.Empty` on a non-2xx status (`:857-862`) and from a catch-all (`:989-993`) that ALSO swallows `OperationCanceledException` (client abort). `GetGrantSetAsync` then caches whatever came back, with no fault check (`:218-224`, fire-and-forget at `:223`). Partial sets are cached too: the org-grant sub-query returns an empty list on non-2xx (`:1044-1050`) or exception (`:1055-1059`), and the junction sub-query likewise (`:1111-1118`, `:1127-1131`) — so a set missing every org grant is stored for 60 s. Scenario: a 429 makes a CIAM partner see zero projects for a minute, and every retry hits the cache.

(b) IDENTITY — `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Ai/Membership/IdentityNormalizationService.cs`, 10 min TTL (`:55`). Each sub-path fails SOFT and the merged `PersonIdentity` is cached regardless (`:174`): systemuser row → `SystemUserData.Empty` so BU/email/oid/primary contact are null (`:230-238`); teams → empty (`:699-707`); contact cross-ref → null (`:568-576`); account → null (`:739-747`); each org resolver → skipped (`:788-797`). Nothing invalidates this cache; `:47-49` says per-user invalidation is a "future Phase 2" channel.

(c) MEMBERSHIP — `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Ai/Membership/MembershipResolverService.cs`, 5 min TTL (`:112`). Builds on the (possibly faulted) identity (`:263`) and caches every response, empty ones included (`:289`, `:325`, `:368`; contact path `:476`, `:501`, `:529`). Its own remarks admit a team-read fault is "indistinguishable from no access" on team-owned records (`:589-592`). Stacking (b)+(c) gives the ~10-15 min worst case (an entry built at the end of the identity's life lives another 5 min).

(d) ACCESS SNAPSHOTS — `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Caching/CachedAccessDataSource.cs`, 60 s (`:59`). Caches whatever the inner source returns (`:131-134`, `:208-210`); it bypasses the cache only for an empty record id or missing token (`:158-161`). The inner `src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseAccessDataSource.cs` converts every fault into an ordinary-looking `AccessRights.None` snapshot: the `GetUserAccessAsync` catch (`:351-369`), a user-lookup fault that is indistinguishable from "no such user" (`:307-320` via `:577-615`), `Denied("exception")` on the record path (`:465-474`), user-lookup non-success/throw on the record path (`:542-548`, `:561-568`), and the read probe returning false on a transport fault exactly as on a 403 (`:497-519`). Three more fault-shaped results the classification must cover: (i) a RetrievePrincipalAccess non-success/unparseable/throw returns null (`:730-741`, `:745-753`, `:759-768`) and the caller degrades to the read probe, which caps rights at Read (`:436-449` record path; `:685-687` document path) — a DEGRADED answer that, if cached, under-grants a Write holder for 60 s; (ii) the document probe treats every non-403/404 status as "no access" (`:828-829`) and swallows exceptions (`:847-851`); (iii) team and role sub-reads on the document path fail soft to empty lists (`:881-884`, `:896-899`, `:917-920`, `:932-935`). `AccessSnapshot` (`src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse/IAccessDataSource.cs:88-102`) has no field that distinguishes any of these from an answer.
DEAD KEYS: `CacheRolesAsync`/`CacheTeamsAsync` (`CachedAccessDataSource.cs:136-139`, `:246-298`; TTL fields `:52-56`) write `sdap:auth:roles:*` / `sdap:auth:teams:*` that nothing in the repo ever reads (grep 2026-09-30: only the writers, the class header `:16-19`, `Infrastructure/DI/SpaarkeCore.cs:104-105` and `docs/architecture/uac-access-control.md:61-62`).

NO BU/TEAM/OWNER INVALIDATION. There are no Dataverse plugins (D-1), so the BFF can only invalidate on its OWN writes; everything else is bounded by TTL. The BFF writes that change teams/BU/owner today (grep 2026-09-30): `Services/Registration/RegistrationDataverseService.cs` — `CreateSystemUserAsync` BU bind (`:355-376`), `AddUserToTeamAsync` (`:421-426`), `RemoveUserFromTeamAsync` (`:467-472`); `Api/ExternalAccess/ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs:609-616` (re-own to the Secure team); `Api/ExternalAccess/UnsecureProjectEndpoint.cs:170-178` (re-own back to a user). The existing invalidation machinery: `IMembershipCacheInvalidator.PublishInvalidationAsync(personId, entity, …)` (`Services/Ai/Membership/IMembershipCacheInvalidator.cs:57-82`) with a SCAN-based subscriber, registered REAL only when `Membership:CacheInvalidator:Enabled` is true AND Redis is registered (`Infrastructure/DI/MembershipModule.cs:193-216`) — default OFF; and `ExternalParticipationService.InvalidateAsync` (`:252-284`) for grant data. The membership cache id embeds an options hash (`MembershipResolverService.cs:1553-1564`), so per-user eviction needs a prefix scan, not a single RemoveAsync. OVER-GRANT DIRECTION: after `ProvisionProjectEndpoint` re-owns a project to the Secure team, BU colleagues keep their ownership-derived membership hit on Teams/SPA until the membership + identity TTLs expire — securing a project does not take effect there for minutes.

(e) A FIFTH CACHE WITH THE SAME STALENESS: `Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ImpersonatedRootSetSource.cs` caches each systemuser's Dataverse-answered root id set per entity type for 5 min (`:86`, key `tenant:{tid}:impersonated-root-set:{systemUserId}:{entityType}` via `:206`). It does NOT cache faults (no catch, `:158-190`) — keep that. But it has the same BU/team/owner staleness, and task 036 (C9, flag-gated swap) makes it THE systemuser-plane source when `ExternalAccess:ImpersonatedRootSets:Enabled` is on: after a re-own, a BU colleague's cached impersonated set still contains the project for up to 5 min. Its only invalidation today is per-user on a POA share change (`InvalidateAsync`, `:220-234`, called from `InternalShareEndpoints.cs:358`, `:473`). Likewise the record-access snapshot (`sdap:auth:record:{entitySet}:{userId}:{recordId}`, `CachedAccessDataSource.cs:173`), which 036's per-record rights amendment may consume, keeps a colleague's pre-re-own rights for 60 s. Both are in scope for the re-own invalidation and the residual table.

INVALIDATION CALLER CONTEXT. `RemoveUserFromTeamAsync` is called from `Services/Registration/DemoExpirationService.cs:183` — a background path with NO HttpContext, so no `tid` claim — while the entries it must evict were written under the affected user's own `tid`. `CreateSystemUserAsync` / `AddUserToTeamAsync` (`DemoProvisioningService.cs:145`, `:159`) and all three writes take an optional `targetDataverseUrl` that can point at a DIFFERENT Dataverse environment, whose users this BFF's caches never hold (D-13: one BFF per customer environment). The eviction must therefore be tenant-segment-agnostic (the existing subscriber already scans `tenant:*`, `MembershipCacheInvalidationSubscriber.cs:275`) and is only meaningful when the write targets this BFF's own `Dataverse:ServiceUrl`.

DEPLOYED CONFIGURATION TODAY. `Membership:CacheInvalidator:Enabled` appears in NO appsettings template and in NO Bicep stack (grep 2026-09-30 over `infrastructure/bicep/**` and `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/appsettings*`), while the BFF's other app settings (e.g. `Redis__Enabled`) are set in `infrastructure/bicep/customer.bicep`, `stacks/model1-shared.bicep` and `stacks/model2-full.bicep`. So the real invalidator is off in every deployed environment.

A PLAUSIBLE, UNTRACED OVER-GRANT (session 26 C12, "not traced end to end"): on the systemuser plane the deny-veto subject is the identity's contact, or else an email-resolved contact (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1017-1034`). If the identity cached a NULL contact because the systemuser-row read faulted, and the email fallback also fails, `ResolveDenyVetoAsync` gets no subject and returns an empty denied set (`:608-612`) — the ethical wall is skipped for membership-term access for up to the identity TTL. A genuinely unlinked systemuser (7 of 8 active interactive users in dev have no `sprk_primarycontact`, session 27 live fact) takes the same code path LEGITIMATELY, so the fix must distinguish "faulted" from "absent".

ADR-009 COMPLIANCE GAP IN THE SAME CLASS. `CachedAccessDataSource` uses `IDistributedCache` directly with `sdap:`-prefixed, non-tenant keys (`:94`, `:173`). ADR-009's operational MUSTs require `ITenantCache` for Sprk.Bff.Api unless the site is allow-listed in `Infrastructure/Cache/SystemCacheKeys.cs`; it is not (no entry names this site; grep 2026-09-30). `docs/architecture/caching-architecture.md:64` and `:98-99` claim the key is already `spaarke:tenant:{tenantId}:auth:access:…` — the code disagrees; `docs/architecture/uac-access-control.md:57` records the truth. See the ADR tensions block.

WHAT IS NOT THIS TASK. The junction-read fault signal and its deny-veto fail direction belong to task 109 (this task CONSUMES 109's outcome to decide "do not cache"). The CIAM-tenant cache-invalidation miss and grant-cache keying belong to the C5 revocation task. The grant cache's DirectAccessLevel drop is task 131. `ImpersonatedRootSetSource` and `MembershipEndpoints` already cache positively only (session 26 counter-examples: `ImpersonatedRootSetSource.cs:158-190`, `MembershipEndpoints.cs:453-475`) — use them as the reference for fault handling and do not change it; ImpersonatedRootSetSource DOES gain the re-own/team eviction and the TTL decision in (e).</background>
