    <residual-staleness status="PROPOSED — requires owner sign-off before merge">
      After this task, the only staleness left is a change made OUTSIDE the BFF (Dataverse admin UI, MDA Assign/Change BU, flows, imports), because without plugins the BFF cannot observe it. Proposed bounds (the executor confirms the final numbers and records them in the notes and docs):
      1. External grant set (sprk_externalrecordaccess, contact/org rows): TTL 60 s, unchanged. Invalidated by the BFF grant/revoke/close/expiry writes (CIAM-tenant miss fixed by the C5 task). Outside-BFF edits: visible within 60 s.
      2. Identity (BU, teams, primary contact, account, organizations): TTL 10 min → proposed 2 min. Invalidated by the BFF registration team/BU writes. Outside-BFF changes (Change BU, team membership edits): visible within 2 min.
      3. Membership resolution (which roots a systemuser matches by owner/team/BU/assignment): TTL 5 min → proposed 2 min. Invalidated with the user's identity and by the BFF re-own paths. Outside-BFF owner/assignment changes: visible within identity TTL + membership TTL = 4 min worst case (stacking).
      4. Access snapshots (RetrievePrincipalAccess answers, per user and record): TTL 60 s, unchanged. Invalidated for ALL users of a record by the BFF re-own paths (per-record eviction). Outside-BFF share/owner changes: visible within 60 s.
      5. Impersonated root sets (ImpersonatedRootSetSource; the systemuser-plane source once 036's flag is on): TTL 5 min → proposed 2 min, to match membership. Invalidated per user on POA share changes (existing), per user on the BFF team add/remove, and for ALL users of the entity type on the BFF re-own paths. Outside-BFF owner/team/BU changes: visible within 2 min.
      6. Fault-derived results: never cached; if a short negative TTL is chosen for throttling protection, at most 10 s, justified in the notes.
      Doc constraint: `docs/architecture/caching-architecture.md:335` already says "MUST: Keep authorization cache TTLs at 2 minutes or less"; identity (10 min), membership (5 min) and impersonated root sets (5 min) violate it today. The proposed bounds comply; any owner-approved bound above 2 min must amend that line in the same change.
      Direction of the residual: after a removal (team, owner, share, BU) the old access persists for at most the bound above (over-grant direction); after an addition, new access appears within the same bound (under-grant direction).
    </residual-staleness>
  </context>

  <adr-tensions>
    <tension adr="ADR-009 (operational MUSTs, 2026-06-26)" status="surface per CLAUDE.md §6.5 before changing the key scheme">
      Rule: "MUST access the distributed cache through the ITenantCache wrapper from Sprk.Bff.Api. Direct IDistributedCache … calls in Sprk.Bff.Api/ are prohibited except for sites enumerated in SystemCacheKeys.cs" and "MUST embed tenant ID in every cache key produced by application code." Conflict: CachedAccessDataSource (a pre-existing site this task rewrites) uses IDistributedCache directly with non-tenant `sdap:auth:*` keys and is not allow-listed. Proposed path: C (pivot to comply) — move its two remaining keys onto ITenantCache with the caller's tid (the same claim resolution IdentityNormalizationService.GetTenantId uses) and the existing subject discrimination (userId + resource/record). Alternative A (project-scoped exception): allow-list in SystemCacheKeys with a three-question justification — rejected as the default because the data is per-caller authorization data, which is exactly what the tenant segment exists to separate, and allow-listing requires architecture review. Impact of C: new key shape orphans existing entries (60 s TTL — harmless); any call path without a tid claim must be identified — see escalation.
    </tension>
    <tension adr="ADR-003 A1 vs .claude/constraints/auth.md" status="doc drift, not a code conflict">
      `.claude/constraints/auth.md:58` ("MUST cache UAC snapshots per-request only") and `:98` ("MUST NOT reuse UAC snapshots across requests") still state the rule ADR-003 Amendment A1 RETIRED (`.claude/adr/ADR-003-authorization-seams.md:69`: "Short explicit TTL across requests is OK; decisions still never cached"). This task keeps cross-request snapshot caching at 60 s under A1. Path C: comply with the current ADR; the stale constraint lines must be corrected in the MAIN session (sub-agents cannot write .claude/, CLAUDE.md §3). A1's own rationale (`ADR-003-authorization-seams.md:71`, "already caches … at 2 min / 60 s") is a historical statement — do not rewrite it; if touched, annotate that the 2-min keys were removed by this task.
    </tension>
    <tension adr="ADR-038 §7 B1 (no transport-level test doubles)" status="surface per CLAUDE.md §6.5; reviewer approval required for any path A use">
      Rule: B1 bans `Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;`; this project reads it as banning transport interception generally (`docs/adr/ADR-038-testing-strategy.md:636-644`). Conflict: criteria 1, 2, 4 and 9 are about how a real non-2xx / 429 / timeout / exception is CLASSIFIED inside `ExternalParticipationService.QueryGrantSetAsync` (+ its two sub-queries) and inside `DataverseAccessDataSource`. Both call `HttpClient.SendAsync` inline, so without a seam the only way to exercise those branches is a transport double — and a test that instead overrides `QueryGrantSetAsync` / `GetGrantSetAsync` / the whole inner `IAccessDataSource` proves only the cache gate, never the classification (a change that does nothing to the classifier would pass it). Proposed path: C (pivot to comply) for ExternalParticipationService — give each Dataverse read a narrow `internal virtual` seam that returns the raw outcome (status + rows, or the exception) and keep the status→answer/fault mapping in the real code, so a subclass feeds a 429/500/timeout into the production classifier (the subclass-seam convention this class already uses). Path A (project-scoped exception) for DataverseAccessDataSource branches that cannot be reached without a transport double: a hand-written scripted handler, following the stated path-A precedent in `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/DataverseRecordShareWireTests.cs:22-31` (task 104), with the exception stated in the test file's remarks and approved in code review. Alternative rejected: overriding the query methods wholesale (does not test the defect). Impact of A: one test file with a hand-written handler; no Moq transport mocks.
    </tension>
    <tension adr="ADR-032 (kill-switch default direction)" status="decide during execution; record">
      If the invalidation this task wires relies on IMembershipCacheInvalidator, the real implementation is registered only when Membership:CacheInvalidator:Enabled=true (default false). A default-off invalidator makes criteria 13-15 true only on paper. Either turn it on for deployed configuration — which means the BFF app settings in `infrastructure/bicep/customer.bicep`, `stacks/model1-shared.bicep`, `stacks/model2-full.bicep` (plus their compiled .json) AND the dev App Service, not only appsettings.template.json, which deployed environments do not read for this key (symmetric Null-Object registration preserved; deploy checklist updated) — or invalidate through ITenantCache/Redis directly on these paths so correctness does not depend on the flag. Record which, and why. Either way a Null-Object peer that silently evicts nothing in an environment where Redis IS enabled must log that fact once at startup (an inert invalidation must be visible).
    </tension>
  </adr-tensions>

  <justification>
    <component name="fault indicator on existing result types (ExternalGrantSet, PersonIdentity resolution result, AccessSnapshot)">
      <existing>The repo already models "could not be read" as a first-class field rather than an empty value: `ActiveOrgMemberships(…, bool Unreadable)` (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:457-464`), `ReferencedOrganizations(…, bool Unreadable)` (`ExternalCallerContext.cs:176-188`), `RootRecordFlags.Unreadable` (`ExternalCallerContext.cs:149`). No equivalent exists on ExternalGrantSet, the identity result or AccessSnapshot (grep 2026-09-30).</existing>
      <extension>Extend the existing types with an additive fault property following that pattern — no new service, interface or result wrapper. If a public HTTP contract would change (PersonIdentity is serialized inside MembershipResponse for GET /api/users/me/memberships), keep the fault signal internal (not serialized) instead of widening the contract.</extension>
      <cost-of-doing-nothing>A 429 on the grant query is cached as "no grants" for 60 s (partner sees zero projects; retries hit the cache); a teammembership fault hides every team-owned matter for up to ~15 min; a transient RetrievePrincipalAccess fault denies the record for 60 s — each fails a concrete request that would have succeeded one second later.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
    </component>
    <component name="BFF write-path invalidation hook for identity + membership (team/BU/owner changes)">
      <existing>`IMembershipCacheInvalidator.PublishInvalidationAsync(personId, entity, …)` (`IMembershipCacheInvalidator.cs:77`) + `MembershipCacheInvalidationSubscriber` (SCAN eviction); `ExternalParticipationService.InvalidateAsync` (`:252`). No identity-cache invalidation exists (`IdentityNormalizationService.cs:47-49`).</existing>
      <extension>Extend IMembershipCacheInvalidator (and its Null peer) with the per-user and per-entity eviction this needs, and add identity-key removal alongside it (the identity key is deterministic: systemUserId, under every tenant segment). Reuse ImpersonatedRootSetSource's existing `CacheId` / `CacheTenantFor` builders (`ImpersonatedRootSetSource.cs:200-206`) for its eviction rather than re-typing the key. A new service is justified only if the extension provably cannot carry it — record why in ≤2 sentences.</extension>
      <cost-of-doing-nothing>After ProvisionProjectEndpoint re-owns a project to the Secure team, BU colleagues keep Teams/SPA access to it for up to identity TTL + membership TTL (today ~15 min), or for the impersonated root-set TTL (5 min) once 036's flag is on; after RemoveUserFromTeamAsync the removed user keeps team-owned records for the same window.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
    </component>
  </justification>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-003 / spec NFR-01">Scope: every consumer of a faulted result. Behaviour for the CURRENT request stays fail-closed exactly as today (a faulted read contributes nothing; a veto subject that could not be read denies). This task changes what is STORED, not what a faulted request is allowed to do — with one exception, criterion 16, which tightens a fail-open.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 (binding)">Scope: fault caching. A fault-derived result is NOT written to the cache. A short negative TTL is permitted only for throttling protection (429 storms), capped at 10 s, and only with the rationale recorded in the task notes.</constraint>
    <constraint source="IdentityNormalizationService.cs:639-645 (canonical)">Scope: cancellation. Rethrow `OperationCanceledException` only when the CALLER's token is cancelled (`when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)`); an HttpClient timeout surfaces as TaskCanceledException with the caller's token NOT cancelled and is a FAULT (not cached, fail-closed for the request). Apply this to every catch this task touches, including the currently unguarded `catch (OperationCanceledException) { throw; }` arms in IdentityNormalizationService (`:226`, `:564`, `:695`, `:735`, `:784`, and the cache helpers `:818`, `:851`), ExternalParticipationService's catch-alls (`:989`, and the sub-queries `:1055`, `:1127`, which today also swallow cancellation), and DataverseAccessDataSource's catch-alls (`:351`, `:465`, `:510`, `:561`, `:610`, `:759`, `:847`, `:896`, `:932`), which today turn a caller abort into a cached-looking None.</constraint>
    <constraint source="classification rule">Scope: fault vs answer. A successful read that returns nothing is an ANSWER and IS cached (a contact with zero grants; a user with no teams; a probe that returns 403/404; a user lookup that succeeds and finds no systemuser; a RetrievePrincipalAccess that answers with an empty rights string). A non-2xx other than a definitive authorization/not-found answer, a throttle (429), a 5xx, a timeout, a deserialization failure or any exception is a FAULT. A DEGRADED answer — rights produced by the read-probe fallback because RetrievePrincipalAccess gave no answer — is treated as a fault for caching purposes (returned to this request unchanged, never cached), because caching it pins a Write holder at Read for the TTL. Write the rule once per cache, next to the code, and test both sides.</constraint>
    <constraint source="task 109 / task 043 single-snapshot invariant">Scope: the junction read. Do not add a second junction query and do not change 109's fail directions; read 109's Unreadable outcome to decide only whether the grant set is cacheable.</constraint>
    <constraint source="task 131">Scope: the cached grant shape. If this task adds anything to CachedGrantSet, bump ExternalParticipationService.CacheVersion again and extend 131's shape-parity guard; never cache the fault flag itself (a faulted set is never written). A fault property added to ExternalGrantSet will trip 131's guard by design: add it to the guard's named exclusion list with the reason ("a faulted set is never written, so the flag is always false on a hit"), and add a test asserting a hit never reports faulted.</constraint>
    <constraint source="stale fault entries at deploy">Scope: identity and membership cache versions. Entries written by the pre-fix code may already be fault-derived and would survive the deploy for up to their old TTL (10 / 5 min). Bump IdentityNormalizationService.CacheVersion (1 → 2) and MembershipResolverService.CacheVersion (4 → 5) in this change so no pre-fix entry is served; the CachedAccessDataSource key migration (ADR-009 path C) flushes the snapshot namespace by construction.</constraint>
    <constraint source="invalidation caller context">Scope: every eviction this task adds. It must work with NO HttpContext (DemoExpirationService is a background path) — i.e. evict across tenant segments (`tenant:*`, as `MembershipCacheInvalidationSubscriber.cs:275` already does) or resolve the tenant explicitly, never silently no-op for lack of a `tid` claim. A write whose `targetDataverseUrl` names a different environment than this BFF's `Dataverse:ServiceUrl` needs no eviction (D-13: this BFF never caches that environment's users); record that rule next to the call. Eviction is best-effort and never fails the write (the write has already happened; the TTL is the backstop), and every eviction key/pattern is derived from the SAME builder the reader uses (TenantCache key format + configured InstanceName + the reader's own id builder), never a hand-typed string.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-1 (no plugins) / ADR-036 / ADR-052">Scope: invalidation. Invalidate only from BFF write paths. If background detection of outside-BFF changes is ever proposed (e.g. a change-tracking poll), it must be an ADR-036 IScheduledJob per ADR-052 — and it is NOT part of this task unless the owner rejects the residual bound (escalation).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-032 / bff-extensions.md §F.1">Scope: DI. Any change to invalidator registration keeps symmetric real/Null-Object registration; no asymmetric `if (flag) register`.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11">Scope: new surface. Extend the existing types and IMembershipCacheInvalidator as described in the justification block; no new service, interface or cache wrapper without its own three-question justification in the task notes.</constraint>
    <constraint source="Spaarke.Dataverse is shared">Scope: AccessSnapshot. The fault indicator is additive with a default of "not faulted"; every existing constructor site keeps compiling and behaving the same. Check every other consumer of IAccessDataSource/AccessSnapshot (grep) and record that none changes behaviour.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038 §7">Scope: tests. No Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;, no DI-registration tests, no ctor null-check tests, no reflection into privates. Reach fault paths through seams that leave the CLASSIFIER under test: for ExternalParticipationService, the production-service subclass pattern overriding only the narrow per-read seams described in the ADR-038 tension (NEVER `QueryGrantSetAsync` or `GetGrantSetAsync` in the tests for criteria 1-5 — that bypasses the classification those criteria are about); a substituted IDataverseService for IdentityNormalizationService/MembershipResolverService; a recording/faulting inner IAccessDataSource for CachedAccessDataSource's cache gate (the `RecordingInnerSource` pattern in AccessCacheCharacterizationTests.cs:49-80). DataverseAccessDataSource's own fault classification has no seam besides its HttpClient (existing constructions: `tests/integration/seam/Auth/ConfidentialClientMigrationSeamTests.cs:63`) — use the path-A hand-written handler per the ADR-038 tension, or a narrower internal seam if one can be introduced without changing behaviour; never Moq. KEEP path: `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/`. No sleeps; seed or read the cache directly for determinism (the approach documented at AccessCacheCharacterizationTests.cs:27-29).</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this task's PR (BFF + Spaarke.Dataverse). Placement decision stated (in-place changes to existing BFF caches and the shared access data source; no new endpoint/package). Publish size vs a FRESH master worktree from a SHORT path, both zipped with Compress-Archive, equal file counts, both absolute sizes + delta reported, ≤60 MB. No new HIGH CVE. Tests updated per bff-extensions.md § F.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-10 / 2026-09-30 (binding)">Scope: live verification. No Dataverse test in CI. The live gate is MANUAL, uses existing non-admin test users in their current BU, and changes RECORD ownership only — never a user's BU or team membership.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-30 (binding)">Scope: completeness. All four caches, the dead keys, the invalidation, the TTLs and the documented residual are delivered here. Scope discovered during execution that belongs to C12 is added, not deferred.</constraint>
  </constraints>

