  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor FULL. Confirm 131 and 109 are merged and read what they shipped. Re-verify every anchor in the background; record corrections in the notes.</step>
    <step order="1">Write the fault-vs-answer classification for each cache (constraint "classification rule") into the task notes before coding; it is the specification for the tests.</step>
    <step order="2">Grant set: signal faults (non-2xx, exception, timeout, org-grant sub-read fault, 109's junction Unreadable) on the returned set; skip the cache write for a faulted set; guard the cancellation catch.</step>
    <step order="3">Identity: record per-sub-path faults; do not cache an identity with any faulted sub-path; expose the fault to consumers internally without changing the /api/users/me/memberships response contract. Membership: do not cache a response built on a faulted identity.</step>
    <step order="4">Deny-veto subject: trace the faulted-identity path end to end and record the trace. If confirmed, make a FAULTED contact resolution on the systemuser plane deny every candidate (mirroring ActiveOrgMemberships.Failed), while a successfully-read unlinked systemuser keeps today's behaviour.</step>
    <step order="5">Access snapshots: add the additive fault indicator to AccessSnapshot; classify every DataverseAccessDataSource fault path, including the RPA-fallback degraded answer, the probe's other-status and catch arms, and the team/role sub-reads; make CachedAccessDataSource skip caching faulted snapshots; delete CacheRolesAsync/CacheTeamsAsync, their TTL fields and header lines; resolve the ADR-009 tension per the reviewed path.</step>
    <step order="6">Invalidation: extend IMembershipCacheInvalidator (per user, and per root entity type for re-own) plus identity-key removal, ImpersonatedRootSetSource eviction (per user on team add/remove; all users of the entity type on re-own) and access-snapshot eviction for the re-owned record (all users); call it from RegistrationDataverseService team add/remove and BU bind (when the write targets this BFF's own environment), ProvisionProjectEndpoint re-own and UnsecureProjectEndpoint re-own; make the path live in deployed configuration (ADR-032 tension); expose it as the ONE hook later owner-changing writers (C10: 144, 146, 148, 149) must call, and say so in its XML doc.</step>
    <step order="7">TTLs: set identity, membership and impersonated-root-set TTLs to the proposed bound; update every comment/doc that states the old values (IMembershipCacheInvalidator.cs:23-24, :44; MembershipCacheInvalidationSubscriber.cs:41, :310, :317; ImpersonatedRootSetSource.cs:85-86; SpaarkeCore.cs:103-105; docs/architecture/caching-architecture.md:64-65, :98-99, :335, :349; docs/architecture/uac-access-control.md:55-67, :121). Record the Dataverse read-volume effect of the shorter TTLs (reads per resolve × expected resolves) in the notes.</step>
    <step order="8">Write the residual-staleness table (final numbers) into docs/architecture/caching-architecture.md and the task notes, and obtain the owner's sign-off (escalation trigger 1) before merge.</step>
    <step order="9">Tests per the closed set; perturbations; build; full BFF unit suite; ArchTests; publish size; CVE check.</step>
    <step order="10">Deploy to dev (bff-deploy skill) and run the MANUAL live gate (criterion 21).</step>
    <step order="11">Record deviations in projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/task-132-access-cache-faults-and-staleness.md; list the .claude/constraints/auth.md:58/:98 correction for the main session; update TASK-INDEX.md status to `✅ [done]`.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>OWNER SIGN-OFF (required before merge): present the final residual-staleness table (context block) and the TTL numbers. If the owner does not accept the outside-BFF bound, STOP — the next option is a Dataverse change-detection IScheduledJob (ADR-036/052), which is a design decision, not an implementation detail.</trigger>
    <trigger>If shortening the identity/membership TTLs to the proposed bound would more than double Dataverse read volume on the Teams/SPA hot path (per the step-7 estimate), STOP and present the trade-off with a recommended bound rather than choosing one.</trigger>
    <trigger>If any CachedAccessDataSource call path has no tid claim available (background/app-only "sp" mode), the ADR-009 path C migration cannot key it by tenant. STOP and present path A (allow-list with justification) vs a sentinel, with a recommendation.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the deny-veto trace (step 4) shows the fail-open is real but the only way to close it would also deny the legitimately unlinked systemusers (no successful-read signal available), STOP — denying 7 of 8 dev users is an access-removal event needing owner sign-off.</trigger>
    <trigger>If distinguishing a fault in DataverseAccessDataSource requires changing its public behaviour for a consumer outside the BFF (another project referencing Spaarke.Dataverse), STOP and report the consumer and the change.</trigger>
    <trigger>If task 109 has not merged when this task starts, do not implement a junction-fault signal here; wait or escalate — a second implementation would re-create the two-snapshot hazard 109 exists to prevent.</trigger>
    <trigger>If treating the RPA-fallback (read-probe) answer as uncacheable would, per the step-7-style estimate, more than double RetrievePrincipalAccess volume during an RPA outage (every request re-running RPA + probe), STOP and present the trade-off (uncached degraded answer vs a ≤10 s negative TTL for the degraded answer) with a recommendation; do not silently cache it for 60 s.</trigger>
    <trigger>If per-entity eviction (all users' membership / impersonated-root-set entries for one entity type) requires a Redis SCAN on a hot write path whose measured cost is unacceptable (record keyspace size and SCAN time in dev), STOP and present the alternatives (per-entity version stamp in the key; pub/sub fan-out via the existing subscriber) with a recommendation, rather than dropping the per-entity eviction.</trigger>
  </escalation>

